<div id="qsrc">
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="1"><p>What is a cation?</p><ol><li>A negative ion formed when an atom gains electrons</li><li>A positive ion formed when an atom loses electrons</li><li>A neutral atom</li><li>A shared pair of electrons</li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="2"><p>An atom gains 2 electrons. What is the charge on the ion?</p><ol><li>2+</li><li>1−</li><li>2−</li><li>0</li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="3"><p>What is the formula of aluminium oxide?</p><ol><li>AlO</li><li>Al<sub>3</sub>O<sub>2</sub></li><li>AlO<sub>3</sub></li><li>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub></li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="0"><p>What is the formula of barium nitrate?</p><ol><li>Ba(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub></li><li>BaNO<sub>3</sub></li><li>BaNO<sub>32</sub></li><li>Ba<sub>2</sub>NO<sub>3</sub></li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="2"><p>What is an ionic bond?</p><ol><li>A shared pair of electrons</li><li>Attraction between delocalised electrons and positive ions</li><li>Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions</li><li>A weak force between molecules</li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="1"><p>Why does solid sodium chloride not conduct electricity?</p><ol><li>It has no charged particles</li><li>Its ions are not free to move</li><li>Its electrons are not free to move</li><li>Its molecules are neutral</li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="mc" data-a="3"><p>Which of these is a 2− ion?</p><ol><li>Nitrate</li><li>Hydroxide</li><li>Ammonium</li><li>Carbonate</li></ol></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="long" data-m="3"><p>Describe what happens to the electrons when calcium reacts with chlorine to form calcium chloride.</p><ul><li>Calcium atom <b>loses 2 electrons</b></li><li><b>Each</b> of two chlorine atoms <b>gains 1 electron</b> / electrons transferred from Ca to Cl</li><li>Forms Ca<sup>2+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> ions (both with full outer shells)</li></ul></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="long" data-m="3"><p>Explain why sodium chloride has a high melting point.</p><ul><li>Giant ionic lattice</li><li><b>Strong electrostatic attraction</b> between <b>oppositely charged ions</b></li><li>Lots of energy needed to overcome the many attractions</li></ul></div>
  <div class="q" data-type="long" data-m="2"><p>Draw a dot-and-cross diagram for sodium fluoride, NaF. Show outer electrons only.</p><ul><li>[Na]<sup>+</sup> with no outer electrons shown</li><li>[F]<sup>−</sup> with 8 outer electrons: 7 dots + 1 cross; brackets and charges on both ions</li></ul><p class="tip">Check it with NaF in the explorer above.</p></div>
</div>

